---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const app = appBySlug('sendlane')!;
const path = '/apps/sendlane';
const title = 'Sendlane after the Privy acquisition: account continuity';
const desc = 'Separate the current commercial offer from the flows, permissions and service commitments your existing Sendlane account depends on.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← App procurement directory</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Operating review · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">

    <h2>What changed, and what to ask next</h2>
    <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy announced its acquisition of Sendlane on February 2, 2026</a>. The announcement describes an email and SMS business with hands-on support and a connected-platform direction. It does not establish the migration schedule or contract terms for every account.</p>
    <p>An existing customer needs an account-specific continuity plan. A new buyer needs the current Privy offer, not an assumption that a historic Sendlane plan can still be purchased unchanged. Ask which product you will log into, who invoices it, which features are available now, and who owns the transition. Keep the written answer with the renewal record.</p>
    <h2>Build a continuity register before renewal</h2>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Dependency</th><th>Evidence to collect</th><th>Decision it supports</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Commercial agreement</td><td>Renewal date, minimum spend, usage rates, cancellation terms and service scope</td><td>Renew, renegotiate or arrange an exit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sending identity</td><td>Domains, authentication, SMS number ownership and registration responsibility</td><td>Keep identity or schedule a controlled replacement</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Active journeys</td><td>Trigger, delay, branch, exit and pending audience for each flow</td><td>Continue, rebuild or deliberately retire each journey</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Permissions</td><td>Channel status, capture evidence, opt-outs and suppression exports</td><td>Determine which recipients remain eligible</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Service commitments</td><td>Named owner, response expectations, migration tasks and escalation path</td><td>Assign work between vendor and store staff</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Do not reduce the register to a feature checklist. A journey can exist in both systems yet behave differently when an order arrives during a delay. Record the behavior your store needs, then ask the account team to demonstrate it in the destination environment.</p>
    <h2>Rehearse one flow with the account team</h2>
    <p>Use a proposed cart recovery rehearsal: create a test cart, enter a delayed reminder, complete the purchase, then inspect the pending message. Repeat with an opted-out number and with email permission only. The acceptance record should show the triggering event, recipient eligibility, cancellation event and final queue state. These are test instructions, not reported Sendlane results.</p>
    <p>If the service team builds the flow, have your internal owner explain it back using the actual conditions. Record who can change it after handover. Hands-on assistance is useful only if the store can maintain the resulting workflow or has a clear ongoing service agreement.</p>
    <h2>Transition without duplicate messages</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Export a dated snapshot of contacts, channel permissions, suppression records and flow definitions available to your account.</li>
      <li>List live forms, integrations and scheduled broadcasts that still feed the existing environment.</li>
      <li>Assign each journey a single sending owner and a cutover time. Account for contacts already waiting inside delays.</li>
      <li>Reconcile destination suppression before enabling any audience import for sending.</li>
      <li>Test purchase exits and reply routing, then release an intentionally limited eligible audience.</li>
      <li>Keep a rollback record: what can be paused, where new permissions are recorded, and who can restore the agreed operating state.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Acquisition alone is not a reason to rebuild a working program. Continue where the account team can substantiate continuity and the operating controls meet your needs. Compare alternatives when unresolved access, support or commercial terms prevent reliable operation. The <a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy route</a> provides a related product entry; confirm current details directly rather than treating an older review as your contract.</p>
    <h2>Budget for the transition work</h2>
    <p>Request a quote at your actual engaged audience, email sends and destination-specific SMS volume. Separate subscription, message usage, carrier charges, implementation and ongoing service. Ask whether overlap billing applies while accounts coexist and whether export or migration work is included. Avoid comparing a software-only price with a managed-service proposal without assigning an internal cost to the missing work.</p>
    <h2>Questions for an existing account</h2>
    <p><strong>Must every customer move immediately?</strong> The acquisition announcement is not an account migration notice. Get your account timeline and any feature changes in writing.</p>
    <p><strong>Can flows simply be copied?</strong> Treat copied content and equivalent execution as separate tasks. Reconcile event names, permissions, delays, exclusions and purchase exits before allowing the replacement to send.</p>

    <h2>Catalog acceptance brief</h2>
    <p>{app.shopifyFit}</p>
    <p>This is a proposed evaluation, not an observed merchant test or a claim that the platform passed it. Save the evidence, unresolved exceptions and responsible owner before approving a change.</p>
    <h2>Continue the decision</h2>
    <p>Return to the <a class="link" href="/apps">procurement directory</a> to compare operating requirements. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> for your actual quote and the <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination guide</a> when more than one sender remains active.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
